course: dictionary
lesson: 1688
title: Tiny threads that carry instructions
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A chromosome is a tiny thread-like structure found inside the centre of a living cell, called the nucleus. It is made of DNA, and it carries genes, which are the instructions that tell a living thing how to grow and work.
explain: Think of a chromosome as a long set of instructions packed tightly into a small space, a bit like a long recipe folded up in a box. «DNA is packed into tiny threads called chromosomes» tells you exactly this: the DNA is wound up into threads so that it fits inside each cell.
explain: Most humans have 46 chromosomes in each cell, arranged in 23 pairs. One of each pair comes from the mother and one from the father, which is why children often look like their parents.
fact: A chromosome is a tiny thread-like structure inside the nucleus of a living cell.
fact: Chromosomes are made of DNA and carry genes, the instructions for how a living thing grows and works.
fact: Humans usually have 46 chromosomes in each cell, in 23 pairs.
fact: The X and Y chromosomes are involved in whether a baby is usually born a boy or a girl.
fact: Great apes have 48 chromosomes, because long ago two ape chromosomes joined into one, our chromosome 2.
exercise: What is a chromosome? | A type of bacteria | A large bag of water inside a cell | A kind of plant food | *A tiny thread-like structure inside the centre of a living cell
exercise: How many chromosomes do humans usually have in each cell? | 64 | 23 | 48 | *46
exercise: One chromosome of each pair comes from the mother. Where does the other one come from? | Nowhere, it is made by the cell | *The father | The cell wall | The grandparents
test: What do chromosomes carry? | *Genes, the instructions for how a living thing grows and works | Water and air | Light for the cell | Only sugar
test: In what pattern do humans usually have their chromosomes? | In 10 pairs | In 48 pairs | In one long line of 46 | *In 23 pairs
exam: Which chromosomes are involved in whether a baby is usually born a boy or a girl? | Chromosome 2 only | The 46 and 48 chromosomes | The nucleus and the cell wall | *The X and Y chromosomes
